encoding     bytes                        len  mark?
utf-8        49 44                          2
utf-8-sig    ef bb bf 49 44                 5  signature EF BB BF
utf-16       ff fe 49 00 44 00              6  BOM FFFE
utf-16-le    49 00 44 00                    4
utf-16-be    00 49 00 44                    4
utf-32       ff fe 00 00 49 00 00 00 44 00 00 00  12  BOM, 4 bytes of it

utf-16 with no suffix WRITES A BOM and picks an order for you; the -le
and -be forms write neither, because you already said which. That is the
whole job of the mark: a value wider than one byte has to go into the
file in some order, and U+FEFF first lets the reader work out which.

UTF-8 HAS NO ORDER TO RESOLVE, AND GETS THE MARK ANYWAY
   utf-8      49 44
   utf-8-sig  ef bb bf 49 44   <- the same three bytes, every time
   Bytes cannot be in the wrong order when the unit IS a byte. Those
   three bytes are a SIGNATURE -- a label saying 'this is UTF-8' -- and
   Windows tools write it so Notepad can tell UTF-8 from a code page.

AND HERE IS THE CSV BUG, IN FULL
   file starts   ef bb bf 49 44 2c 6e 61
   read as utf-8      first column '\ufeffID'
   read as utf-8-sig  first column 'ID'
   equal to 'ID'?     False and True

   The first column is not called ID. It is called ZERO WIDTH NO-BREAK
   SPACE followed by ID, and it draws as 'ID' on every screen you will
   look at. row['ID'] raises KeyError, the header LOOKS right in the
   error message, and this is the single most reported bug in this
   chapter. The fix is to name the encoding utf-8-sig when reading a
   file that might have come from Excel -- it strips the mark if it is
   there and does nothing if it is not.
